{#
  The demo's own post layout.

  This is the whole override mechanism in one file: the site ships
  `themes/demo/layouts/post.njk` and its `site.json` says `"theme": "demo"`, so
  the packaged `layouts/post.njk` is never read again, and every other template
  — the base layout, the home archive, the tag pages, the 404 — still comes
  from `@geekity/cms` and still gets updates when the package does.

  It extends the packaged base layout by name, so the site does not own the
  <head>, the header or the footer either. What it adds is a byline and a
  reading time, which is what makes the override visible in a browser.

  The byline is the demo's own markup. The packaged theme's is
  `partials/bio.njk`, the whole `p-author h-card` the andrewshell.org design
  puts in an entry's footer (TASK-83); this site wants a line under the title
  instead, so it writes the `h-card` itself and reads the same object the
  packaged bio would. `author` is the post's author as a profile rather than as
  a string (TASK-67): `name` to print, and `url` to link to their archive when
  the name is one of the site's users. A post that names nobody falls back to
  the site's own author, which is a name and nothing else.

  This layout renders no bio and loses nothing by it: the site menu is in the
  header on every page (TASK-105), so an override is free to draw the words
  however it likes without taking the navigation with it.
#}
{% extends "layouts/base.njk" %}
{% import "partials/tags.njk" as taxonomy with context %}

{% set writer = author or { name: site.author } %}
{% set minutes = ((content | striptags | wordcount) / 220) | round(0, "ceil") %}

{% block content %}
<article class="post h-entry">
  <header class="post-header">
    {#- A note has no name, so it has no heading either: its words are it. -#}
    {% if postType != "note" %}
    <h1 class="post-title p-name">{{ title }}</h1>
    {% endif %}
    <p class="post-byline">
      by
      <span class="p-author h-card">
        {%- if writer.url %}
        <a class="u-url" href="{{ writer.url | url }}">{{ writer.name }}</a>
        {%- else %}
        {{ writer.name }}
        {%- endif %}
      </span>
    </p>
    <p class="post-meta">
      {% if date %}
      <time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ date | date("iso") }}">{{ date | date }}</time>
      {% if updated %}
      &middot; updated
      <time class="dt-updated" datetime="{{ updated | date("iso") }}">{{ updated | date }}</time>
      {% endif %}
      &middot;
      {% endif %}
      {{ minutes }} minute read
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="post-body e-content">
    {{ content | safe }}
  </div>

  {% if tags.length or categories.length %}
  <footer class="post-footer">
    {{ taxonomy.categories(categories) }}
    {#- The tag macro prints the same paragraph of `p-category` links the
        categories are (TASK-83). The demo wants its tags hashed and in small
        caps, so it wraps them and styles the wrapper. -#}
    <div class="post-tags">{{ taxonomy.list(tags) }}</div>
  </footer>
  {% endif %}
</article>

{#
  The replies, likes and boosts, from the packaged partial: a layout that
  overrides this file keeps the conversation by including it, and `conversation`
  is only on the context when there is one.
#}
{% if conversation %}
{% include "partials/conversation.njk" %}
{% endif %}

{#
  And the comment form, the same way: only on the context when the post is
  still taking comments, so this is the whole of what a site has to keep.
#}
{% if commentForm %}
{% include "partials/comment-form.njk" %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
